[
  "[SCENE: Cam on desk setup, MacBook visible]\nSo, this sticker.\n[SHOW: Close-up of Dead Rabbit Radio sticker on MacBook lid]\nRight there on the lid of the MacBook Air, next to the Apple logo.\nMost people assume it's podcast merch. It's not.\nIt's a physical artifact of the most humbling technical experiment I ever ran.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about where you are right now, what's on the desk, how it feels to be recording this]\nThis is The Sound Above: Minus One. I'm Mike Hall.\nTonight I'm going to walk you through what I built, how it went from idea to 1,000+ transcriptions, and why I ghosted it, and what I learned.\nFive Raspberry Pis. Over a thousand podcast episodes. Early ChatGPT, before there were proper tools.\nAnd a podcaster named Jason Carpenter who was open to the idea.\n[PAUSE: let it breathe]\nThe lessons from that experiment are running on this machine right now.\nThat's the reason we're here.\n",
  "[SCENE: Cut to archive footage or slide: SCNA 2011 photo if available]\nTo get why this experiment happened, you have to go back to 2011.\nI was recording technical interviews for UGtastic. Started at SCNA 2011.\nBuilt-in mic on a 2010 MacBook Pro. Apple Photo Booth. That's it.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about what it was like recording at conferences - the ambient noise, hauling gear, the improvised setup]\nOver the next few years I recorded more than two hundred conversations.\nAnd when I was done, I hit a wall.\nIn 2013, human transcription cost one to two dollars a minute.\nFor two hundred-plus interviews, that's a four-figure bill for an unfunded grassroots project.\nThe cloud speech APIs were worse. They'd spit back phonetic gibberish.\nNHibernate came back as \"and hibernate.\" StructureMap as \"structure map\" with a space.\n[SHOW: Example bad transcript snippet if you have one]\nFor ten years, those tapes sat on hard drives. Knowledge locked in digital amber.\n",
  "[SCENE: Terminal or browser tab]\nThen the world shifted. Late 2022, early 2023.\nOpenAI open-sourced Whisper. Georgi Gerganov released whisper.cpp.\nThe marginal cost of transcription dropped to zero overnight.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about what that moment felt like - the realization that the thing you'd been waiting a decade for was now just... free]\nBut I didn't want to risk the UGtastic tapes on unverified tools.\nI needed a massive, clean dataset first. Something I could break things on.\nI needed a single consistent speaker. Hundreds of hours. No crosstalk. No panel noise.\n[PAUSE]\nThat's how I found Dead Rabbit Radio.\n[SHOW: deadrabbitradio.com in browser]\n",
  "[SCENE: Show the fluffle GitHub repo in browser or terminal]\n[SHOW: github.com/just3ws/fluffle]\nDead Rabbit Radio. Jason Carpenter. Daily paranormal folklore. Solo host.\nZero crosstalk, no mic swaps, consistent cadence.\nAnd honestly? His speaking rhythm is a lot like mine.\nI had five Raspberry Pi boards in my home lab. node01 through node05.\n[FREEFORM: Describe the physical setup - where the Pis sat, what the basement looked like, how janky the whole rig was]\nI wrote bin/distribution in Ruby to bin-pack the audio files across nodes by byte size.\nbin/distribute pushed files via Net::SSH and rsync.\nOn each Pi, bin/transcribe ran whisper.cpp with a mandatory 120-second thermal cooldown between jobs.\n[SHOW: The actual bin/transcribe script open in terminal or editor]\nNo heatsinks. Just stock boards. Sixty seconds of compute, two minutes of cooling. Repeat.\n",
  "[SCENE: Back on cam]\nFor several weeks that cluster just hummed in my basement.\nIt generated 2,034 distinct transcript files.\nText, subtitles. Over a thousand episodes.\nI reached out to Jason on Discord to ask his blessing.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about that conversation - how it felt to reach out, what Jason said, why it mattered to get that blessing before shipping anything]\nHe was genuinely gracious. Kind. Supportive. More than I expected.\nAnd then I hit the cliff.\n[PAUSE: this is the hard part, let it land]\nI tried to turn fluffle into a universal podcast aggregator.\nImmediately: broken RSS feeds. Corrupted ID3 tags. Erratic audio formats everywhere.\nAt the same time, high-severity production incidents at the day job.\nObservability architecture work that was consuming everything I had.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about what working memory exhaustion actually feels like - not as a clinical term, but as a lived thing. The tab-switching. The feeling of losing the thread.]\nI told myself I was an imposter playing with toy scripts.\nI froze. Stopped committing. Stopped responding to Jason on Discord.\nFor months I couldn't even bring myself to open the repo or listen to the show.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show the running pipeline dashboard or ztranscribe output in terminal]\nFor a long time I carried fluffle as a private regret.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about what changed - when you picked it back up, what you were able to see from a distance that you couldn't see up close]\nBut craftsmanship teaches you to inspect your scars honestly.\nThat experiment wasn't a failure. It was the essential skunkworks lab that made today possible.\nIt taught me: normalize audio deterministically before running inference.\nIt taught me Maildir queue architecture. It taught me lexical priming for proper nouns.\nAnd it proved local hardware sovereignty works.\n[SHOW: ztranscribe running on a UGtastic interview file, timing output visible]\nFive Raspberry Pis taking two hours per episode.\nNow it's Apple Silicon. Ninety seconds. All 218 UGtastic interviews: transcribed and searchable.\n[PAUSE]\nTo Jason Carpenter: thank you. For the stories, the generosity, and the craft.\nThe sticker stays on the laptop.\nThe lessons are in every line of code this system runs today.\n[SCENE: Outro card / Patreon link]\nIf you want to support the archive and the independent hosting behind it:\npatreon.com/whoistechcmty\nNext up: Episode Zero. Minus Zero. The Mirage of Competence.\nKeep your feedback loops fast.\n"
]